7. WHAT THE SOFTWARE DOES WITH YOUR DATA

Drakar runs the `claude` and `codex` you installed, in the folders you open, and
shows what they do. Nothing leaves your Mac by way of Drakar itself.

Three honest limits on that sentence:

  (a) The agents are the vendors' programs. They talk to Anthropic and OpenAI
      over your own sign-in, read and change your files as you allow them to, and
      have their own network behaviour and their own terms. What they send is
      between you and those vendors.

  (b) Drakar reads what those agents write under `~/.claude`, any
      `~/.claude-<name>` beside it, and `~/.codex`, and writes there only when you
      ask it to: installing or removing a skill, moving a folder's sessions to
      another account, and the lock file a `claude --ide` in a terminal looks for
      while the editor bridge runs. Skills installed from git are cloned under
      `~/.agents/skill-sources`.

  (c) So that sessions outlive the app, a helper inside it keeps the agents
      running and logs what passes between them and Drakar, under Application
      Support. A session's log is deleted once it has ended and Drakar has read
      its end. None of it leaves your Mac.

8. WHAT IT ASKS OF YOUR SYSTEM

Drakar ships with no entitlements at all, on the app or on its helper, and the
audit asserts that. It is not sandboxed, because the agents it starts work on
your files and read their own settings in your home folder. It asks for no
Accessibility or other privacy permission.

It listens on two sockets and opens neither to the network: the editor bridge, on
127.0.0.1 only, which answers only a client holding the token it writes, readable
by you alone, into Claude Code's lock file; and its helper's, a Unix socket only
your own Drakar can reach. `SECURITY.md` in the repository says how to check both.

9. UPDATES

Updates within your major version are free.

Unlike the rest of the app, the update check is a network call, and it is the
only one. It is off on a fresh install and stays off until you answer the
question. Turning it on is a standing preference in Settings ▸ Updates; "Check
Now…" there is a single check you asked for and leaves the standing preference
alone. No identifier is sent with either, and nothing is installed without asking.

